'''''Argemone ochroleuca''''' is a species of [[Argemone|prickly poppy]], a [[flowering plant]] commonly known as '''pale Mexican prickly poppy''' or '''Mexican poppy'''. It is native to Mexico and is also an introduced weed in many temperate and tropical regions of the world.{{r|fna}} It can grow up to {{cvt|1|m}} in height and has a sticky yellow sap.

==As an introduced species==
It can be found as an introduced species in [[Western Australia]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://florabase.dpaw.wa.gov.au/browse/profile/2961|title=FloraBase—the Western Australian Flora|last=Western Australian Herbarium|first=Biodiversity and Conservation Science|website=florabase.dpaw.wa.gov.au|language=en|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref> New Zealand,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nzor.org.nz/names/2639941d-5218-4d2f-b77c-10b66e1dc8f5|title=NZOR Name Details - ''Argemone ochroleuca'' Sweet|website=www.nzor.org.nz|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref> the Arabian Peninsula, Iran, the Indian subcontinent,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.flowersofindia.net/catalog/slides/Pale%20Mexican%20Poppy.html|title=''Argemone ochroleuca'' - Pale Mexican Poppy|website=www.flowersofindia.net|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref> South Africa,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.ville-ge.ch/musinfo/bd/cjb/africa/details.php?langue=an&id=176432|title=CJB - African plant database - Detail|website=www.ville-ge.ch|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref> and [[Arizona]] in the southwestern United States.<ref name=fna>{{Cite web|url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=233500138|title=''Argemone ochroleuca'' in Flora of North America @ efloras.org|website=www.efloras.org|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:671710-1|title=''Argemone ochroleuca'' Sweet|website=Plants of the World Online|access-date=2020-03-16}}</ref> It has become invasive in Australia, Africa, tropical Asia, New Zealand and a number of oceanic islands. Since ''Argemone ochroleuca'' produces a large number of seeds, it can accidentally be introduced into new areas as a [[seed contaminant]]. It is often a problem in [[agricultural land]], but also has the potential to outcompete native species and decrease [[biodiversity]]. It is also toxic to humans and livestock.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Argemone ochroleuca (pale Mexican pricklypoppy) |url=https://www.cabi.org/isc/datasheet/112405#tosummaryOfInvasiveness |access-date=2022-04-17 |website=www.cabi.org |language=en}}</ref> 

